; The overworld (docs/world-map.md). One per project.
;
; Records are lowercase; the grid below is not. One glyph per terrain kind, as
; declared by catalog/terrain.cat — there is no palette record here, because
; there is only one world and terrain needs no per-level ordering.
;
; start      where a new game puts the party.
; area       a rectangle overriding its terrain's difficulty. Tested in FILE
;            ORDER, last match wins — author broad regions first and carve
;            exceptions after them.
; location   something the party can enter. Names a DUNGEON (dungeons.cat),
;            never a level stem.

start 6 6

; The settled south: the road and the farmland either side of it.
area lowlands 2 5 20 11 difficulty=0.12
; The moors north of the hills, where the barrows are. Authored AFTER lowlands
; and overlapping nothing — the ordering rule matters the day they do.
area moors 2 1 12 4 difficulty=0.45

location dungeon waystation 10 10 level=showcase entryx=13 entryz=21
; THE BACK WAY: the same dungeon, a different door. It stands somewhere
; else on the map and comes out somewhere else inside — which is why a
; location names its dungeon and its landing spot rather than being one.
location dungeon waystation_back 5 13 dungeon=waystation level=level2 entryx=9 entryz=4

